 Resolve the immutable source revision before rendering. The cache key includes clip ID, revision, and renderer version. Concurrent misses for one key share a job ticket. Once rendering completes, subsequent requests can receive the JPEG directly. Failed jobs are removed from the deduplication map so a later request can retry.
 
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q (A1 FIFO + Am LRU + ghost list) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU lets one long sequential screening evict useful comparison sheets. Aged frequency counters retain repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance, can keep stale favorites too long, and lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, protecting comparison sheets during big screenings at the cost of two queues, a ghost list, and a tunable size split — all private to this worker. |
+| Budget split | A1 3 GiB / Am 9 GiB, fixed queues (no cross-queue eviction) | Single 12 GiB pool with cross-queue eviction | Fixed queues keep eviction decisions local and simple; the split is a tuning constant, revisitable without structural change. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy is 2Q — protects reused comparison sheets from one-off screening scans; extra bookkeeping accepted.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Tune the A1/Am split (3/9 GiB) and the ghost-list bound (2048 keys) against observed workloads after the pilot.
+
 ## Private Cache
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses the 2Q policy. The cache splits the 12 GiB budget into two queues: a first-use queue A1 capped at 3 GiB, and a main queue Am capped at 9 GiB. A1 is managed FIFO; Am is managed LRU. A keys-only ghost list of at most 2048 keys remembers recently evicted A1 entries and holds no JPEG data.
+
+Admission and movement:
+
+- A rendered sheet whose key is not in the ghost list is admitted to the tail of A1. If this would push A1 past 3 GiB, A1's head entry is evicted and its key enters the ghost list.
+- A sheet in A1 that is accessed again before eviction is promoted to Am (most-recently-used position), leaving A1.
+- A miss whose key is in the ghost list is treated as a reuse: on render completion the sheet is admitted directly to Am and the key leaves the ghost list. This is what shields reused sheets from one-off screening scans.
+- Admitting into Am that would push Am past 9 GiB evicts Am's least-recently-used entry. When the ghost list reaches 2048 keys, its oldest key is discarded before a new one is added.
+
+The 3 GiB / 9 GiB split and the 2048-key ghost bound are starting values to be tuned against observed screening workloads; tuning changes only these constants. Job tickets, the render queue, the HTTP contract, and the 12 GiB total are unchanged: no other node observes queues, ghost keys, or promotion events, and a failed job never reaches any queue.
+
